window.FM_DATA = window.FM_DATA || {};
window.FM_DATA["math"] = {"id":"math","title":"Math & Formulas","entries":[{"id":"probability-utils","name":"ProbabilityUtils","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Probability/ProbabilityUtils.cs","src_lines":[6,53],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"WeightedPick<T>(IReadOnlyList<(T item, float weight)>)","sig":"public static T WeightedPick<T>(IReadOnlyList<(T item, float weight)> options)","kind":"method","name":"WeightedPick","static":true,"comment":"Picks one item from a list based on relative weights (they don't need to sum to 1) — the standard loot-table pattern: WeightedPick(new[]{(\"Common\",70f),(\"Rare\",25f),(\"Legendary\",5f)})."},{"id":"Chance(float)","sig":"public static bool Chance(float probability01) => Random.value < probability01","kind":"method","name":"Chance","static":true,"comment":"true with the given probability, e.g. Chance(0.25f) for a 25% chance."},{"id":"RollDice(string)","sig":"public static int RollDice(string notation)","kind":"method","name":"RollDice","static":true,"comment":"Parses and rolls simple dice notation (\"2d6\" -> sum of two 1-6 rolls, \"1d20\"/\"d20\" -> one 1-20 roll)."},{"id":"NextGaussian(float, float)","sig":"public static float NextGaussian(float mean = 0f, float stdDev = 1f)","kind":"method","name":"NextGaussian","static":true,"comment":"Box-Muller transform: converts uniform randomness into a normal (bell-curve) distribution — use for \"usually near the average, rarely extreme\" values (loot rolls, stat variance) instead of the flat feel of plain Random.Range."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathFormulas_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Weighted picks, percentage chances, dice-notation rolls and Gaussian values on top of UnityEngine.Random.","problem":"Four things every game needs from randomness, none of which UnityEngine.Random provides directly. Weighted selection from a loot table is the big one — the cumulative-sum loop is short, but it gets rewritten slightly differently in every system that needs it. Chance is a readability wrapper that stops `Random.value < 0.25f` from being written backwards. RollDice lets designers author damage as '2d6' in data rather than as code. And NextGaussian gives values that cluster near an average and rarely go extreme, which is what stat variance and loot rolls usually want — the flat distribution of Random.Range makes a 1 and a 50 equally likely, which reads as noise rather than variation.","workedExample":{"setup":"A three-tier loot table with weights authored as readable percentages, and one specific roll traced through WeightedPick.","inputs":[{"name":"options","value":"[(\"Common\", 70f), (\"Rare\", 25f), (\"Legendary\", 5f)]"},{"name":"roll","value":"80 (a sample value from Random.Range(0f, total))"}],"steps":["total = 70 + 25 + 5 = 100.","roll = Random.Range(0f, 100f); suppose it comes back 80.","First option: cumulative = 0 + 70 = 70. Is 80 <= 70? No — move on.","Second option: cumulative = 70 + 25 = 95. Is 80 <= 95? Yes — return \"Rare\".","The band that maps to Rare is (70, 95] — 25 units wide out of 100, exactly its share of the total weight.","If the loop somehow falls through (floating-point accumulation error), the last option is returned as a fallback rather than throwing."],"result":"\"Rare\". The weights are relative, not percentages: passing 7 / 2.5 / 0.5 gives identical odds, and adding a fourth entry silently rebalances everything else rather than requiring you to re-normalize by hand.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Loot tables and drop tables, where designers author relative weights rather than percentages that must sum to 100.","Enemy or event spawn selection weighted by biome, difficulty or wave number.","Tabletop-style damage and skill checks authored as strings in a ScriptableObject: RollDice(\"2d6\"), RollDice(\"1d20\").","Stat variance on generated items — NextGaussian(mean, stdDev) so most rolls land near average and perfect rolls are genuinely rare.","Simple percentage gates: Chance(0.25f) for a quarter-chance bonus, spawn, or barks."],"usage":{"code":"if (ProbabilityUtils.Chance(0.25f)) SpawnBonus();\n\nint damage = ProbabilityUtils.RollDice(\"2d6\");\n\nvar table = new (string, float)[] { (\"Common\", 70f), (\"Rare\", 25f), (\"Legendary\", 5f) };\nstring rarity = ProbabilityUtils.WeightedPick(table);\n\nfloat hp = Mathf.Max(1f, ProbabilityUtils.NextGaussian(mean: 50f, stdDev: 10f));","codeNote":"WeightedPick<T> takes an IReadOnlyList<(T item, float weight)>, so any array or List of value tuples binds directly — no wrapper struct required."},"apiNotes":{"Chance(float)":"Random.value < probability01. Chance(0f) is always false; Chance(1f) is always true (Random.value is in [0,1)). Purely a readability wrapper.","RollDice(string)":"Lowercases, splits on 'd', and sums that many Random.Range(1, sides + 1) rolls. \"2d6\" is two six-sided dice; \"d20\" and \"1d20\" are both one twenty-sided die. Returns the SUM, not the individual rolls.","NextGaussian(float, float)":"Box-Muller: sqrt(-2 * ln(u1)) * sin(2*pi*u2), scaled by stdDev and offset by mean. u1 is taken as 1 — Random.value specifically so it can never be 0 and make the log undefined."},"gotchas":[{"title":"RollDice parses with int.Parse and validates nothing","body":"\"2d6+1\", \"2 d 6\", \"d%\" and an empty string all throw FormatException at runtime, inside whatever gameplay call happened to hit them. Only a bare NdM or dM form works. If the notation comes from a designer-edited asset or user input, wrap the call or validate the string when the asset loads, not when the dice are rolled."},{"title":"RollDice with zero sides silently returns the die count","body":"\"2d0\" becomes two calls to Random.Range(1, 1), which returns 1 each time, for a total of 2. No exception, no warning — just a constant where you expected randomness. Negative sides behave similarly oddly."},{"title":"A zero-weight entry in first position can still be picked","body":"The comparison is `roll <= cumulative`, and Random.Range(0f, total) can return exactly 0. If the first option has weight 0 its cumulative is also 0, and 0 <= 0 matches. Vanishingly rare, but real. Filter zero-weight entries out, or at least never put them first."},{"title":"NextGaussian is unbounded","body":"About 0.3% of samples land beyond three standard deviations, and there is no clamp anywhere in the function. NextGaussian(50f, 20f) will occasionally return a negative number. Clamp at the call site — a Mathf.Max or Mathf.Clamp — rather than assuming the distribution stays inside a sensible range."},{"title":"Everything here uses the global UnityEngine.Random stream","body":"None of these functions take a seed or an RNG instance, so results are not reproducible and are perturbed by anything else in the frame that consumes UnityEngine.Random. For procedural generation, replays or shared daily seeds, use SeededRandom — and note it has no weighted-pick or dice equivalent, so you would have to write those yourself."},{"title":"WeightedPick allocates nothing but iterates twice","body":"One pass to total the weights, one to find the band. Fine for a table of a few dozen entries called a few times a second; if you are picking thousands of times per frame from a static table, precompute the cumulative array yourself."},{"title":"WeightedPick throws on an empty list","body":"ArgumentException, by design — an empty table has no meaningful answer. Check Count before calling if a table can legitimately be emptied at runtime."}],"seeAlso":["RandomBag","PityTimer","SeededRandom","ItemRegistry","StatCurves"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":["PityTimer"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"seeded-random","name":"SeededRandom","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Probability/SeededRandom.cs","src_lines":[9,59],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Seedable, instance-based PRNG (xorshift32) — independent of UnityEngine.Random's single global stream, so the SAME seed always reproduces the SAME sequence regardless of what other code calls UnityEngine.Random in between. Use for anything that needs to be reproducible: procedural generation from a level seed, replays, daily-challenge seeds shared between players.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"SeededRandom(int)","sig":"public SeededRandom(int seed)","kind":"ctor","name":"SeededRandom","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"NextFloat()","sig":"public float NextFloat() => (NextUInt() >> 8) * (1f / 16777216f)","kind":"method","name":"NextFloat","static":false,"comment":"[0, 1) — genuinely exclusive of 1. The obvious `NextUInt() / (float)uint.MaxValue` is NOT: it reaches exactly 1f when NextUInt() returns uint.MaxValue, and float's 24-bit mantissa rounds a whole band of high values up to 1f as well — which breaks the standard `(int)(r.NextFloat() * count)` indexing idiom with an IndexOutOfRange. Taking the top 24 bits and scaling by 2^-24 caps the result at 16777215/16777216 = 0.99999994f, which is exactly representable and provably below 1. No precision is lost: a float never held more than 24 meaningful bits of the original 32 anyway."},{"id":"NextFloat(float, float)","sig":"public float NextFloat(float min, float max) => min + NextFloat() * (max - min)","kind":"method","name":"NextFloat","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"NextInt(int, int)","sig":"public int NextInt(int min, int max) => min + (int)(NextUInt() % (uint)Mathf.Max(1, max - min))","kind":"method","name":"NextInt","static":false,"comment":"[min, max) — matches UnityEngine.Random.Range's int overload convention (max exclusive)."},{"id":"NextBool(float)","sig":"public bool NextBool(float chance = 0.5f) => NextFloat() < chance","kind":"method","name":"NextBool","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"NextVector2(float, float)","sig":"public Vector2 NextVector2(float min, float max) => new Vector2(NextFloat(min, max), NextFloat(min, max))","kind":"method","name":"NextVector2","static":false,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathFormulas_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Instance-based xorshift32 PRNG: the same seed always replays the same sequence, independent of UnityEngine.Random.","problem":"UnityEngine.Random is one global stream shared by every script in the project, which makes it useless for anything that must be reproducible. Seed it for your dungeon generator and the particle system, an animation event and a third-party asset all pull from the same stream in between, so the same seed produces a different dungeon on the next run — or on a different machine, or after you add one Random.value call somewhere unrelated. A SeededRandom is its own stream: nothing else can touch it, so a level seed, a replay, or a daily challenge shared between players regenerates identically every time. xorshift32 is chosen because it is four integer operations with a single uint of state — cheap enough to create thousands of them, and small enough to reason about.","workedExample":{"setup":"Why the constructor hashes the seed instead of using it directly — traced through the raw xorshift first, then through the hash that now runs before it.","inputs":[{"name":"seed","value":"1"}],"steps":["WITHOUT hashing, state would start at 1. state ^= state << 13 gives 1 ^ 8192 = 8193.","state ^= state >> 17: 8193 >> 17 is 0, so state stays 8193.","state ^= state << 5: 8193 << 5 = 262176. The two values share no set bits, so the XOR is a plain sum: 8193 + 262176 = 270369.","Seeds 2 and 3 follow the identical no-overlap path and yield 540738 and 811107 — exactly 2x and 3x. The first draw was proportional to the seed.","WITH hashing, the seed is avalanched first: 1, then ^= >>16 leaves 1, then *0x7feb352d = 2146121005, then ^= >>15 = 2146159355, then *0x846ca68b = 1753872457, then ^= >>16 = 1753845952.","xorshift then runs on 1753845952 and returns 145099912; seed 2 gives 289945872 and seed 3 gives 3078990264."],"result":"First NextFloat() for seeds 1, 2 and 3 is now 0.033784, 0.067508 and 0.716883 — unrelated values. Across 50,000 sequential one-shot seeds the mean is 0.4997 and all ten deciles land within 1.5 percent of even. Before the hash was added those same three seeds gave 0.000063, 0.000126 and 0.000189, so a one-shot new SeededRandom(levelIndex).NextFloat() was barely random at all.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Procedural level generation from a level seed, so the same seed always produces the same dungeon, cave or maze.","Daily-challenge or weekly-run seeds that must produce identical content for every player.","Replays and deterministic simulation, where random decisions have to be reproduced exactly on playback.","Deterministic decoration: give each chunk or room a SeededRandom keyed on its coordinates and it regenerates identically without storing anything.","Tests that need repeatable randomness without the global state of UnityEngine.Random leaking between cases."],"usage":{"code":"var rng = new SeededRandom(levelSeed);\nint roomCount   = rng.NextInt(4, 9);          // 4..8, max exclusive\nVector2 jitter  = rng.NextVector2(-2f, 2f);\nif (rng.NextBool(0.3f)) PlaceTreasure();\nfloat scale     = rng.NextFloat(0.8f, 1.2f);","codeNote":"Draw in a fixed order — the sequence is reproducible, but only if every run consumes values in the same order. An early-out or a conditional draw will desynchronise everything after it."},"apiNotes":{"NextFloat()":"Takes the top 24 bits of NextUInt() and scales by 2^-24, which caps the result at 16777215/16777216 = 0.99999994f. Genuinely [0, 1): the obvious NextUInt() / (float)uint.MaxValue reaches exactly 1f, and that is what this used to do. No precision is lost — a float never held more than 24 of the 32 bits.","NextFloat(float, float)":"min + NextFloat() * (max - min). Since NextFloat() is strictly below 1, max is never returned.","NextInt(int, int)":"min + NextUInt() % (max - min), with max EXCLUSIVE to match UnityEngine.Random.Range's int overload. The range is passed through Mathf.Max(1, ...), so it never throws.","NextBool(float)":"NextFloat() < chance, defaulting to a fair coin. NextBool(0f) is always false and NextBool(1f) is always true, both exactly, because the range of NextFloat() is closed at 0 and open at 1.","NextVector2(float, float)":"Two independent NextFloat(min, max) draws, so it consumes TWO values from the stream, not one. A rectangle sample, not a disc — it is not uniform over a circle."},"gotchas":[{"title":"The seed is hashed, so a given seed no longer matches older builds","body":"The constructor avalanches the seed (the lowbias32 finalizer) before xorshift ever runs, which is what makes sequential seeds independent. It also means every seed now produces a DIFFERENT sequence than it did before this fix: a level, dungeon or daily challenge generated from a stored seed will not reproduce content generated by an older build. Seeds in save files still replay consistently from here on — they just describe different content than they used to."},{"title":"Reproducibility still depends on a fixed draw order","body":"Hashing fixes the quality of the stream, not the discipline of consuming it. The sequence replays identically only if every run draws the same values in the same order, so an early-out, a conditional draw, or a reordered loop desynchronises everything after it."},{"title":"NextInt never throws, even for invalid ranges","body":"Mathf.Max(1, max - min) means NextInt(5, 5) and NextInt(5, 0) both return 5 rather than erroring. A range bug therefore shows up as a suspiciously constant value rather than an exception. There is also the usual modulo bias when (max - min) does not divide 2^32 evenly, which is negligible for gameplay-sized ranges."},{"title":"The state cannot be read, saved or restored","body":"`state` is private with no accessor, so you cannot snapshot a generator mid-sequence into a save file. To resume, store the original seed plus how many values were consumed, and re-draw them on load — which also means your generation code has to consume a deterministic count."},{"title":"Not thread-safe","body":"NextUInt mutates a single uint field with no synchronisation. Two threads sharing one instance will interleave reads and writes and destroy reproducibility. Give each thread or job its own instance, seeded deterministically."},{"title":"Deliberately minimal API","body":"There is no NextVector3, no shuffle, no weighted pick and no Gaussian. ProbabilityUtils has those, but every one of them uses the global UnityEngine.Random and cannot be pointed at a SeededRandom — so a reproducible weighted pick has to be written by hand on top of NextFloat()."}],"seeAlso":["ProbabilityUtils","RandomBag","NoiseUtils","CaveGenerator","MazeGenerator","NameGenerator"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":[],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"random-bag","name":"RandomBag<T>","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Probability/RandomBag.cs","src_lines":[10,38],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"\"Shuffle bag\" randomizer (the classic Tetris 7-bag technique): guarantees every item is drawn exactly once before the bag reshuffles and repeats, instead of letting pure independent random rolls occasionally streak the same item many times in a row. Use for anything where players would notice and feel cheated by an unlucky streak — which piece/card comes next, which enemy type spawns.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"RandomBag(IEnumerable<T>)","sig":"public RandomBag(IEnumerable<T> items)","kind":"ctor","name":"RandomBag","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Draw()","sig":"public T Draw()","kind":"method","name":"Draw","static":false,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathFormulas_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Shuffle-bag draw: every item comes out exactly once before any of them repeats, which removes unlucky streaks.","problem":"Uniform independent rolls are fair in the long run and infuriating in the short run. Draw from four enemy types 20 times and you will routinely see the same one three or four times running, and players read that as broken rather than unlucky. The shuffle bag — best known as Tetris's 7-bag randomizer — trades a little unpredictability for a guarantee: fill a bag with one of each item, draw without replacement until it is empty, then refill. Within any cycle the distribution is exactly uniform, the sequence is still unpredictable, and the pathological streak is impossible.","workedExample":{"setup":"A three-item bag drawn six times — two complete cycles.","inputs":[{"name":"items","value":"[\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"]"}],"steps":["The constructor copies the items and calls Refill(), so bag = [A, B, C].","Draw 1: Random.Range(0, 3) picks an index, the item is removed. Say B. bag = [A, C].","Draw 2: Random.Range(0, 2). Say C. bag = [A].","Draw 3: only one entry left, so A is forced. bag = [] (empty).","Draw 4: bag.Count == 0, so Refill() restores [A, B, C] and the draw proceeds. Say C.","Draws 5 and 6 empty the bag again, in some order, over the remaining A and B."],"result":"Across those six draws each of A, B and C appears exactly twice, no matter what the RNG did. The order within a cycle is free, but the counts are not. Note the C at draws 3-adjacent positions: the longest possible run of one item is 2 — the last draw of one bag followed by the first draw of the next.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Falling-piece games: the classic 7-bag tetromino randomizer, so a player never waits ten pieces for an I.","Enemy or wave composition, so every enemy type appears before any repeats.","Card, question or prompt decks in a quiz or party game — no duplicate question until the deck is exhausted.","Ambient barks, idle animations and music tracks, where a repeat two lines apart is immediately noticeable.","Loot from a small curated pool where every item should appear in a predictable cadence."],"usage":{"code":"private readonly RandomBag<string> pieces =\n    new RandomBag<string>(new[] { \"I\", \"O\", \"T\", \"S\", \"Z\", \"J\", \"L\" });\n\nstring next = pieces.Draw();","codeNote":"Draw() is the whole API — it refills automatically when the bag empties, so there is nothing to reset or poll."},"apiNotes":{"Draw()":"Refills first if the bag is empty, then removes and returns one item at a random index. Always returns something; the constructor already guaranteed the source list is non-empty."},"gotchas":[{"title":"Duplicate entries are the only way to weight it","body":"This is not a weighted picker. A bag built from [A, A, B] does give A twice as often as B per cycle, and that is the mechanism — there is no weight parameter. For fine-grained or runtime-adjustable weights use ProbabilityUtils.WeightedPick instead, and accept the streaks that come with it."},{"title":"The maximum run length is 2, not 1","body":"An item drawn last from one bag can be drawn first from the next. If even a back-to-back double must be impossible, you need extra logic on top — reshuffle when the refilled bag's first pick matches the previous draw, or peek and swap."},{"title":"The contents are fixed at construction","body":"The constructor copies the input into its own private List, so mutating the collection you passed in afterwards has no effect, and there is no Add/Remove/Refill API exposed. To change what is in the bag, build a new RandomBag — accepting that the current cycle's guarantee is discarded."},{"title":"You cannot see how much is left","body":"The remaining-items list is private with no count or peek accessor, so a 'next piece' preview or a '3 of 7 left' UI has to track Draw() calls itself. Keep your own counter modulo the item count if you need that."},{"title":"Uses the global UnityEngine.Random stream","body":"Draw order is not reproducible from a seed you control and is perturbed by any other code consuming UnityEngine.Random in the same frame. There is no SeededRandom overload; a deterministic bag would have to be written separately."},{"title":"Empty input throws at construction","body":"ArgumentException from the constructor, which is the right place for it — the failure surfaces where the mistake was made rather than at the first Draw() deep in gameplay. Just be aware that building bags from a filtered collection can throw if the filter matches nothing."}],"seeAlso":["ProbabilityUtils","PityTimer","SeededRandom","ObjectPool"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":[],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"pity-timer","name":"PityTimer","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Probability/PityTimer.cs","src_lines":[11,40],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Bad-luck protection (\"pity timer\"): each failed roll increases the effective chance, guaranteeing a success within guaranteedAfter attempts instead of letting pure independent rolls occasionally streak far longer than feels fair — the classic gacha/loot-box design pattern. Plain C# class (not a MonoBehaviour) so a system with several independent pity counters (e.g. one per loot table) can just hold a field of these rather than needing a GameObject per counter.","src_serialized":[{"name":"baseChance","type":"float","default":"0.05f","tooltip":"Base chance of success on any single roll, before pity increases it.","header":null,"range":null,"order":0,"comment":null},{"name":"guaranteedAfter","type":"int","default":"20","tooltip":"A roll is guaranteed to succeed once this many consecutive failures have accumulated.","header":null,"range":null,"order":1,"comment":null}],"src_members":[{"id":"FailCount","sig":"public int FailCount { get; private set; }","kind":"property","name":"FailCount","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"PityTimer()","sig":"public PityTimer() { }","kind":"ctor","name":"PityTimer","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"PityTimer(float, int)","sig":"public PityTimer(float baseChance, int guaranteedAfter)","kind":"ctor","name":"PityTimer","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Roll()","sig":"public bool Roll()","kind":"method","name":"Roll","static":false,"comment":null},{"id":"Reset()","sig":"public void Reset() => FailCount = 0","kind":"method","name":"Reset","static":false,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathFormulas_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Bad-luck protection: each failed roll raises the effective chance until a success is guaranteed.","problem":"Independent random rolls produce streaks, and streaks feel like cheating even when the maths is honest. At a 5% drop rate, roughly one player in three still has nothing after 20 attempts and one in a hundred has nothing after 90 — and those are the players who post about it. A pity timer keeps the headline odds but puts a hard ceiling on the worst case: every failure nudges the chance up, and after a set number of failures the next roll simply succeeds. It is a plain C# class rather than a MonoBehaviour so a loot system can hold a dozen independent counters (one per table, one per rarity tier) as ordinary fields without spawning a GameObject for each.","params":[{"field":"baseChance","why":"The success chance on a roll with no accumulated failures — the floor the escalation builds from.","tuning":"Set it BELOW your advertised drop rate. Because every subsequent roll is strictly more likely than this, the observed rate always comes out higher than baseChance. For a '5% drop' that actually measures near 5%, start noticeably lower and simulate."},{"field":"guaranteedAfter","why":"The hard ceiling: once this many consecutive failures have accumulated, the next roll succeeds outright. It also sets how fast the chance ramps, since the escalation is FailCount / guaranteedAfter.","tuning":"Pick it from the player experience you want to cap, not from the maths — 'nobody should ever go more than 20 pulls dry'. Small values (under about 5) make the ramp so steep that baseChance barely matters; 1 or 0 make every roll succeed."}],"workedExample":{"setup":"The default configuration — a 5% base chance with a 20-attempt guarantee — traced as failures accumulate.","inputs":[{"name":"baseChance","value":"0.05"},{"name":"guaranteedAfter","value":"20"}],"steps":["Below the guarantee, chance = baseChance + (1 - baseChance) * FailCount / guaranteedAfter.","FailCount 0:  0.05 + 0.95 * 0/20  = 0.05    (5%)","FailCount 1:  0.05 + 0.95 * 1/20  = 0.0975  (9.75%)","FailCount 5:  0.05 + 0.95 * 5/20  = 0.2875  (28.75%)","FailCount 10: 0.05 + 0.95 * 10/20 = 0.525   (52.5%)","FailCount 19: the branch `FailCount >= guaranteedAfter — 1` fires first, so chance is forced to 1.0 — the formula's own 0.9525 is never used.","On a success FailCount resets to 0; on a failure it increments."],"result":"The 20th roll after 19 straight failures always succeeds, so no player can ever go more than 20 attempts without a hit. Note the halfway point is already better than a coin flip — the average number of rolls per success is far below 20, and the realised drop rate is well above the 5% baseChance.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Gacha and loot-box pulls where a published pity count is part of the design contract with players.","Rare enemy drops, so a farming session cannot run dry indefinitely.","Critical-hit or dodge chances in a combat system, to smooth out runs that feel unfair.","Procedural events (a wandering merchant, a treasure room) that should show up eventually rather than statistically.","Any tuned probability where the worst case, not the average, is what generates complaints."],"usage":{"code":"[SerializeField] private PityTimer legendaryDrop = new PityTimer(0.02f, 50);\n\nvoid OnEnemyKilled()\n{\n    if (legendaryDrop.Roll()) SpawnLegendary();\n    else Debug.Log($\"{legendaryDrop.FailCount} kills since the last legendary\");\n}","codeNote":"The two-argument constructor is the only way to configure it in code — baseChance and guaranteedAfter are private [SerializeField] with no setters. Declared as a serialized field, it also shows up in the Inspector because the class is [System.Serializable]."},"apiNotes":{"FailCount":"Consecutive failures since the last success. Read-only from outside (private setter) — useful for a '12 / 20 to guaranteed' UI readout.","Roll()":"Computes the escalated chance, rolls it via ProbabilityUtils.Chance, then either resets FailCount to 0 (success) or increments it (failure). One call per attempt — calling it twice rolls twice.","Reset()":"Zeroes FailCount. The only mutator besides Roll(); use it when a new pity cycle should start (a new banner, a new season, a difficulty change)."},"gotchas":[{"title":"The realised drop rate is always higher than baseChance","body":"Every roll after the first uses a chance strictly greater than baseChance, and the guarantee caps the tail. So if a design document says '5% drop rate', typing 0.05 into baseChance produces something meaningfully more generous. Decide whether baseChance or the observed rate is the number you are balancing against, and simulate a few thousand rolls to check."},{"title":"FailCount cannot be saved or restored","body":"It is an auto-property with a private setter, and it is not a serialized field — so it does not survive a domain reload, does not round-trip through a save file, and there is no API to set it. baseChance and guaranteedAfter (both [SerializeField]) DO persist, which makes the asymmetry easy to miss. If pity progress must survive a session, this class cannot express that as written."},{"title":"guaranteedAfter of 0 or 1 makes every roll succeed","body":"The guard is `FailCount >= guaranteedAfter — 1`. With guaranteedAfter = 1 that is 0 >= 0, and with 0 it is 0 >= -1 — both true on a fresh timer, so chance is forced to 1 every time. Neither value is validated. Keep guaranteedAfter at 2 or more."},{"title":"Roll() draws from the global UnityEngine.Random stream","body":"It calls ProbabilityUtils.Chance, which uses UnityEngine.Random.value. Results are therefore not reproducible from a seed you control, and are affected by anything else in the frame that consumes the global stream. A PityTimer cannot be driven by a SeededRandom without reimplementing Roll()."},{"title":"One counter per independent thing","body":"Sharing a single PityTimer across several loot tables couples them: a hit on one resets the pity for all. Give each table, tier or banner its own instance — which is exactly why it is a lightweight serializable class rather than a component."}],"seeAlso":["ProbabilityUtils","RandomBag","SeededRandom","ItemRegistry"],"src_uses":["ProbabilityUtils"],"src_usedBy":["NameGenerator"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"easing-functions","name":"EasingFunctions","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Easing/EasingFunctions.cs","src_lines":[16,87],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Standard easing formulas. t is expected in [0, 1]; the result usually is too (Back/Elastic/Bounce overshoot on purpose). Kept as plain math, not tied to any tween engine, so it can drive UI animation, camera moves, or shader parameters.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Evaluate(EaseType, float)","sig":"public static float Evaluate(EaseType type, float t)","kind":"method","name":"Evaluate","static":true,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathAndUI_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"One static Evaluate(EaseType, float) implementing 13 standard easing curves, with no tween-engine dependency.","problem":"Linear motion reads as mechanical; the fix is almost always an easing curve, and the same handful of formulas get retyped (usually slightly wrong) in every project. This class is the canonical set as plain float maths — no coroutines, no tween handles, no components. That matters because the same curve is needed in places a tween library cannot reach: a shader parameter, a camera rig's own update loop, a procedural mesh deformation, a progress bar driven by download bytes rather than time. You call Evaluate with a normalized progress value and get back a shaped progress value; what you do with it is your business.","workedExample":{"setup":"OutCubic — the workhorse \"arrives gently\" curve — sampled across a one-second move.","inputs":[{"name":"type","value":"EaseType.OutCubic"},{"name":"formula","value":"1 - Mathf.Pow(1 - t, 3)"}],"steps":["t = 0.25:  1 - (0.75)^3 = 1 - 0.421875 = 0.578125","t = 0.50:  1 - (0.50)^3 = 1 - 0.125    = 0.875","t = 0.75:  1 - (0.25)^3 = 1 - 0.015625 = 0.984375","t = 1.00:  1 - 0        = 1","For contrast, OutBack at t = 0.75 with its constants c1 = 1.70158 and c3 = 2.70158, and t1 = t - 1 = -0.25: 1 + 2.70158*(-0.015625) + 1.70158*(0.0625) = 1 - 0.042212 + 0.106349 = 1.064137"],"result":"A one-second OutCubic move is already 57.8% complete after a quarter of a second and 98.4% complete after three quarters — the motion is front-loaded and settles softly. OutBack, by contrast, deliberately returns 1.064 at t = 0.75, overshooting the target by 6.4% before easing back to 1.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Shaping a manually driven lerp: Vector3.Lerp(a, b, EasingFunctions.Evaluate(ease, elapsed / duration)).","A pop-in for a UI panel using OutBack, where the slight overshoot past full scale is the whole effect.","Feeding a shader property (dissolve amount, fresnel strength) a non-linear ramp without an AnimationCurve asset.","Camera moves and dolly transitions that must feel hand-animated rather than mechanical.","Anywhere a designer's chosen EaseType value needs to become an actual number."],"usage":{"code":"float k = EasingFunctions.Evaluate(EaseType.OutBack, elapsed / duration);\ntransform.localScale = Vector3.LerpUnclamped(Vector3.zero, Vector3.one, k);","codeNote":"LerpUnclamped, not Lerp — Back, Elastic and Bounce return values outside [0,1] on purpose, and plain Lerp would silently flatten the overshoot that is the point of the curve."},"apiNotes":{"Evaluate(EaseType, float)":"Switches on the enum and returns the shaped value. Any case not explicitly handled (including EaseType.Linear) falls through to `default: return t`, so t comes back untouched and unclamped."},"gotchas":[{"title":"Nothing is clamped, on either side","body":"Evaluate never clamps its input or its output. Pass t = 1.5 to InQuad and you get 2.25. Divide elapsed by duration without a Mathf.Clamp01 and an overshooting final frame will send whatever you are driving past its target. Clamp the input yourself if your t can exceed 1."},{"title":"Back, Elastic and Bounce leave the [0,1] range deliberately","body":"OutBack peaks at roughly 1.064; InBack dips below 0; Elastic does both repeatedly. Never feed these results straight into something that must stay in range — a Color channel, a CanvasGroup alpha, a normalized shader property — without clamping at the consumer. Use Mathf.LerpUnclamped / Vector3.LerpUnclamped where you WANT the overshoot."},{"title":"The enum is not a symmetric set","body":"Only OutBounce exists (no InBounce, no InOutBounce), and Elastic has In and Out but no InOut. If you are porting eases from another library by name, three of the combinations you might expect simply are not there."},{"title":"Elastic special-cases exact 0 and 1","body":"ElasticIn and ElasticOut return t unchanged when t == 0f or t == 1f, guarding the Pow/Sin terms at the boundaries. Because that is an exact float comparison, a t of 0.9999999 takes the normal path — which is fine, the formula is continuous there, but do not rely on the special case firing for near-boundary values."}],"seeAlso":["EaseType","Tween","TweenExtensions","MathUtils","StatCurves"],"src_uses":["EaseType"],"src_usedBy":["EaseType","FlyToUIEffect","TargetingStrategies","TweenHandle","TweenLoopMode"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"ease-type","name":"EaseType","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"enum","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Easing/EasingFunctions.cs","src_lines":[5,12],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":false,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[],"src_enumValues":["Linear","InQuad","OutQuad","InOutQuad","InCubic","OutCubic","InOutCubic","InBack","OutBack","InOutBack","InElastic","OutElastic","OutBounce"],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":[],"entryKind":"enum","status":"published","summary":"Enum naming the 13 easing curves EasingFunctions.Evaluate can apply, from Linear to OutBounce.","problem":"Easing is a choice a designer makes, not a formula a programmer hard-codes, so the curve needs to be a value you can put on a serialized field and pick from an Inspector dropdown. This enum is that value. It covers the four families that cover almost every real requirement — Quad and Cubic for plain acceleration, Back for anticipation and overshoot, Elastic and Bounce for springy arrivals — each in the In (slow start), Out (slow finish) and InOut (slow at both ends) flavours where they make sense. Note the set is deliberately incomplete: there is no InBounce, InOutBounce or InOutElastic, so do not assume symmetry when porting values from another tweening library.","useCases":["A [SerializeField] EaseType on a UI animation component, so the curve is picked in the Inspector rather than recompiled.","Storing the curve choice in a ScriptableObject preset shared by every toast, modal and tooltip in the project.","Passing an ease choice through a tween API: Tween.To(..., EaseType.OutCubic).","Driving a shader parameter or camera move with the same named curve the UI uses, so motion feels consistent across systems."],"src_uses":["EasingFunctions"],"src_usedBy":["AccordionPanel","CardCarousel","EasingFunctions","FadeManager","FlyToUIEffect","GlitchEffect","HologramEffect","PullToRefresh"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"stat-curves","name":"StatCurves","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/StatCurves/StatCurves.cs","src_lines":[7,36],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Common gameplay-balancing formulas as pure functions — the kind of curve every RPG/looter needs and usually reinvents slightly differently each time.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"ExperienceForLevel(int, float, float)","sig":"public static float ExperienceForLevel(int level, float baseXP = 100f, float growthRate = 1.15f)","kind":"method","name":"ExperienceForLevel","static":true,"comment":"Total XP required to REACH a given level (level 1 = 0 XP). Growth compounds each level by growthRate."},{"id":"LinearFalloff(float, float, float, float)","sig":"public static float LinearFalloff(float distance, float minDistance, float maxDistance, float fullDamage)","kind":"method","name":"LinearFalloff","static":true,"comment":"Linear falloff: full damage at/inside minDistance, zero damage at/beyond maxDistance."},{"id":"SmoothFalloff(float, float, float, float)","sig":"public static float SmoothFalloff(float distance, float minDistance, float maxDistance, float fullDamage)","kind":"method","name":"SmoothFalloff","static":true,"comment":"Smoothed (ease-out) falloff — damage drops off gently near minDistance and steeply near maxDistance, reading as more \"natural\" than a straight linear ramp."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathFormulas_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Three balancing formulas as pure functions: compounding XP requirements, linear damage falloff, smoothstep falloff.","problem":"Every RPG and every shooter needs the same two or three curves, and every project reinvents them slightly differently — usually inline, usually in the middle of a level-up or damage-application method where nobody can find them again. Pulling them out as pure static functions means the curve is one place, is testable without a scene, and can be graphed in a tool or a spreadsheet to check the balance before anyone plays it. The XP function in particular encodes the non-obvious bit: the closed-form geometric-series sum, including the special case that stops a perfectly reasonable growthRate of 1 from producing NaN.","workedExample":{"setup":"The default XP curve — 100 base XP compounding 15% per level — and what those numbers mean on a balance spreadsheet.","inputs":[{"name":"baseXP","value":"100 (default)"},{"name":"growthRate","value":"1.15 (default)"}],"steps":["Formula: baseXP * (growthRate^(level-1) - 1) / (growthRate - 1) — the sum of a geometric series, so the result is the TOTAL to reach that level, not the cost of it.","Level 1: short-circuits to 0 before any arithmetic.","Level 2: 100 * (1.15^1 - 1) / 0.15 = 100 * 0.15 / 0.15 = 100.","Level 3: 100 * (1.3225 - 1) / 0.15 = 100 * 0.3225 / 0.15 = 215.","Level 4: 100 * (1.520875 - 1) / 0.15 = 347.25.","Level 5: 100 * (1.74900625 - 1) / 0.15 = 499.3375.","The COST of going 4 -> 5 is the difference: 499.3375 - 347.25 = 152.0875, which is exactly 100 * 1.15^3. Each level costs 15% more than the one before it, which is the property the closed form guarantees.","Level 10: 100 * (1.15^9 - 1) / 0.15 = 100 * 2.517876 / 0.15 = 1678.58."],"result":"Reaching level 10 on the defaults takes 1678.58 total XP, of which the 9 -> 10 step alone is 305.90 (that is 100 * 1.15^8). If you need the cost of a single level, subtract two calls — this function will never give it to you directly.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Driving a level-up check and an XP bar from a single authored curve rather than a hand-typed table.","Generating a balance table in an editor tool or a CSV export, so designers can see the whole progression at once.","Explosion and area-of-effect damage that tapers with distance instead of cutting off at a hard radius.","Falloff for non-damage effects too — audio volume, screen shake intensity, knockback strength from a blast centre.","Prototyping progression pacing by changing one growthRate value instead of retyping a hundred-row table."],"usage":{"code":"float needed = StatCurves.ExperienceForLevel(player.level + 1);\nfloat thisLevelCost = needed - StatCurves.ExperienceForLevel(player.level);\n\nfloat dist = Vector3.Distance(blastCentre, target.position);\nfloat damage = StatCurves.SmoothFalloff(dist, minDistance: 2f, maxDistance: 10f, fullDamage: 120f);","codeNote":"ExperienceForLevel returns the cumulative total, so the cost of the current level is always a difference of two calls."},"apiNotes":{"ExperienceForLevel(int, float, float)":"Cumulative XP to REACH a level; level 1 is 0. growthRate exactly 1 is special-cased to the linear limit baseXP * (level - 1) rather than dividing by zero.","LinearFalloff(float, float, float, float)":"Mathf.InverseLerp(maxDistance, minDistance, distance) — arguments reversed on purpose so that closer means higher — times fullDamage, clamped. With min 2, max 10, fullDamage 100, a distance of 7 yields 37.5.","SmoothFalloff(float, float, float, float)":"Same t, then smoothstep t*t*(3 - 2t). The same setup gives 31.64 at distance 7 (steeper than linear out near the edge) but 95.7 at distance 3 against linear's 87.5 (gentler up close) — a wider effective sweet spot near the centre."},"gotchas":[{"title":"ExperienceForLevel returns a cumulative total, not a per-level cost","body":"Compare it against the player's lifetime XP, not against a bar that resets each level. Wiring it to a per-level bar makes early levels look instant and later ones look impossible. Subtract consecutive calls to get the cost of one level."},{"title":"The return type is float, and XP totals outgrow it","body":"Beyond about 16.7 million a float can no longer represent every integer, so high-level totals start snapping to multiples of 2, then 4. With the default 100 base XP and 15% growth the cumulative total crosses that line around level 73. If your XP is stored as int or long, compute the curve in double or cap the level range you feed it."},{"title":"growthRate below 1 is not guarded","body":"Only the exact-1 case is special-cased (via Mathf.Approximately, so near-1 values route there too). A growthRate of 0.9 produces a converging series — the XP requirement approaches a ceiling and later levels become nearly free — and a negative growthRate makes Mathf.Pow oscillate in sign. Neither errors. Keep growthRate above 1 unless you have deliberately designed a decelerating curve."},{"title":"Swapping minDistance and maxDistance silently inverts the falloff","body":"Both falloff functions call InverseLerp(maxDistance, minDistance, distance) with no validation, so passing them the wrong way round produces a curve that deals MORE damage further away. Nothing warns you; the only symptom is that the explosion feels backwards."},{"title":"Both falloffs clamp, so there is no negative or extrapolated damage","body":"Distance at or inside minDistance always returns fullDamage; at or beyond maxDistance it returns 0. That is usually what you want, but it also means the functions cannot express a minimum-damage floor at long range — add that yourself with a Mathf.Max."},{"title":"SmoothFalloff is not simply 'gentler' than linear","body":"It is gentler near minDistance and steeper near maxDistance. The net effect is a larger near-full-damage zone at the centre and a faster drop at the edge, which reads as a punchier blast. If you wanted the opposite — a small sweet spot and a long tail — this is the wrong curve, not a milder version of the right one."}],"seeAlso":["EasingFunctions","MathUtils","Health","ProbabilityUtils","CurrencyWallet"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":[],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"bezier-utils","name":"BezierUtils","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Bezier/BezierUtils.cs","src_lines":[8,33],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Quadratic/cubic Bezier evaluation — camera paths, UI motion along a curve, projectile arcs. Each has a matching Tangent function since orienting something to face along the curve is the most common reason to need the derivative, not just the point.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Quadratic(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 Quadratic(Vector3 p0, Vector3 p1, Vector3 p2, float t)","kind":"method","name":"Quadratic","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"QuadraticTangent(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 QuadraticTangent(Vector3 p0, Vector3 p1, Vector3 p2, float t)","kind":"method","name":"QuadraticTangent","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"Cubic(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 Cubic(Vector3 p0, Vector3 p1, Vector3 p2, Vector3 p3, float t)","kind":"method","name":"Cubic","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"CubicTangent(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 CubicTangent(Vector3 p0, Vector3 p1, Vector3 p2, Vector3 p3, float t)","kind":"method","name":"CubicTangent","static":true,"comment":null}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathFormulas_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Quadratic and cubic Bezier point plus tangent evaluation for camera paths, UI motion and projectile arcs.","problem":"Unity ships AnimationCurve for scalar easing and Vector3.Lerp for straight lines, but nothing for \"move along a curved path in 3D\". Re-deriving the Bernstein polynomial at each call site is where sign errors creep in. Bezier is the right primitive when a human authors the shape by dragging handles: the curve is pulled toward the interior control points without passing through them, which is exactly the feel of a handle. Each evaluator has a matching Tangent function because orienting the thing that travels the curve is the usual reason you need the derivative, not just the position.","workedExample":{"setup":"A simple lobbed arc: launch at the origin, land 10 units away, with a single control point pulled up to y = 10.","inputs":[{"name":"p0","value":"(0, 0, 0)"},{"name":"p1","value":"(5, 10, 0)"},{"name":"p2","value":"(10, 0, 0)"},{"name":"t","value":"0.5"}],"steps":["u = 1 - t = 0.5","u*u*p0 = 0.25 * (0,0,0) = (0, 0, 0)","2*u*t*p1 = 2 * 0.5 * 0.5 * (5,10,0) = 0.5 * (5,10,0) = (2.5, 5, 0)","t*t*p2 = 0.25 * (10,0,0) = (2.5, 0, 0)","Quadratic = (0,0,0) + (2.5,5,0) + (2.5,0,0) = (5, 5, 0)","QuadraticTangent: 2*u*(p1-p0) = 1.0 * (5,10,0) = (5,10,0); 2*t*(p2-p1) = 1.0 * (5,-10,0) = (5,-10,0); their sum is (10,0,0), normalized to (1,0,0)"],"result":"The apex of the arc is (5, 5, 0) — HALF the control point's height, not (5, 10, 0) — and the tangent there is dead level, (1, 0, 0). To peak at y = 10 you must raise the handle to y = 20.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["A cutscene camera that swings around an object instead of dollying in a straight line.","Projectile arcs where the shape is art-directed (a lobbed grenade, a spell bolt) rather than simulated by physics.","UI elements that fly from an inventory slot to a HUD counter along a curve, with the icon rotating to face its direction of travel.","Laying out a curved rope, cable or tentacle by evaluating the same curve at N evenly spaced t values.","The underlying evaluator for SplineUtils, which converts each Catmull-Rom segment into a cubic Bezier and calls straight into Cubic/CubicTangent."],"usage":{"code":"float t = elapsed / duration;\nVector3 pos = BezierUtils.Cubic(p0, p1, p2, p3, t);\nVector3 dir = BezierUtils.CubicTangent(p0, p1, p2, p3, t);\ntransform.SetPositionAndRotation(pos, Quaternion.LookRotation(dir));","codeNote":"Position and orientation from the same t — the reason both halves of each pair exist."},"apiNotes":{"Quadratic(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)":"Three control points, one interior handle. Cheapest curve that bends; the curve touches p0 and p2 only.","QuadraticTangent(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)":"Derivative of Quadratic, returned NORMALIZED — a direction, not a velocity.","Cubic(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)":"Four control points, two interior handles. The standard curve: it can S-bend, which the quadratic cannot.","CubicTangent(Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, Vector3, float)":"Derivative of Cubic, also normalized. Feed it to Quaternion.LookRotation to face along the path."},"gotchas":[{"title":"The interior control points are not on the curve","body":"Only p0 and the final point are touched. A quadratic peaks at exactly half its handle's offset (see the worked example), and a cubic typically reaches about three quarters. If you are placing handles by hand and the curve looks too shallow, that is why — overshoot the handle deliberately."},{"title":"Tangents are normalized, so speed information is thrown away","body":"Both Tangent functions end in .normalized. You cannot use them to derive how fast the follower is moving. You also get Vector3.zero back from a degenerate curve (all control points identical), and Quaternion.LookRotation logs an error on a zero vector — guard with a sqrMagnitude check if the control points can coincide."},{"title":"t is neither clamped nor arc-length","body":"Nothing clamps t to [0,1]; values outside it extrapolate the polynomial, which diverges fast. More importantly, equal steps in t are NOT equal steps in world distance — the curve moves faster through its straight stretches. For constant-speed travel, build an arc-length table with SplineUtils.BuildLengthTable and convert distance to t with SplineUtils.DistanceToT."},{"title":"No allocation, no caching, no state","body":"These are pure functions on Vector3 structs, safe to call in a tight loop or from a job-free Update. There is also nothing to invalidate: change a control point and the next call reflects it immediately."}],"seeAlso":["SplineUtils","SplinePath","SplineFollower","PathBuilder","EasingFunctions"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":["FlyToUIEffect","SplineUtils"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"spline-utils","name":"SplineUtils","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Spline/SplineUtils.cs","src_lines":[15,138],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Catmull-Rom spline through an arbitrary list of points — unlike a single cubic Bezier (which only passes through its two endpoints), this passes through EVERY point, which is what a \"click to place waypoints\" authoring workflow needs: the curve must hit each spot actually clicked. Extends BezierUtils rather than reimplementing curve math — each segment converts its local 4-point Catmull-Rom window into an equivalent cubic Bezier and hands it straight to BezierUtils.Cubic/CubicTangent.  All functions here are pure/stateless (points passed in every call), matching BezierUtils' own style. A caller that evaluates the same point set repeatedly (SplinePath, PathBuilder) should build a length table ONCE with BuildLengthTable and reuse it, rather than rebuilding it on every query.","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Evaluate(IReadOnlyList<Vector3>, float, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 Evaluate(IReadOnlyList<Vector3> points, float t, float tension = 0f)","kind":"method","name":"Evaluate","static":true,"comment":"Evaluates the whole path at t in [0,1], where 0 is the first point and 1 is the last. tension: 0 = a standard (uniform Catmull-Rom) curve; 1 = dead-straight segments between every pair of points, still passing through every one. Values in between loosen/tighten how far the curve bulges past a straight line at each point — this is the \"curvature\" control exposed on SplinePath."},{"id":"EvaluateTangent(IReadOnlyList<Vector3>, float, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 EvaluateTangent(IReadOnlyList<Vector3> points, float t, float tension = 0f)","kind":"method","name":"EvaluateTangent","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"BuildLengthTable(IReadOnlyList<Vector3>, float, int)","sig":"public static float[] BuildLengthTable(IReadOnlyList<Vector3> points, float tension = 0f, int samplesPerSegment = 20)","kind":"method","name":"BuildLengthTable","static":true,"comment":"Samples the whole path at `samplesPerSegment` uniform steps per segment and returns the cumulative distance travelled at each sample — table[0] is always 0, table[last] is the total path length. Build this ONCE per point-set/tension combination and reuse it with DistanceToT rather than resampling from scratch on every query (SplinePath does exactly this, invalidating the cache only when points, tension, or the transform actually change)."},{"id":"TotalLength(float[])","sig":"public static float TotalLength(float[] lengthTable) => lengthTable[lengthTable.Length - 1]","kind":"method","name":"TotalLength","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"DistanceToT(float[], float)","sig":"public static float DistanceToT(float[] lengthTable, float distance)","kind":"method","name":"DistanceToT","static":true,"comment":"Converts a DISTANCE along the path (0 .. TotalLength) into the t value the Evaluate calls in BuildLengthTable used — this is what makes constant-speed movement and even prefab/mesh spacing possible, since t on its own runs at whatever pace the spline math happens to produce, not at a constant rate per unit of world distance."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":"Math/Spline/Editor/SplineUtilsSelfTest.cs","src_demoScene":[],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Catmull-Rom spline through every point, plus an arc-length table so things can travel it at constant speed.","problem":"A cubic Bezier only touches its two endpoints, which is wrong for the workflow that actually matters: a designer clicks waypoints in the scene and expects the curve to pass through each spot they clicked. Catmull-Rom does exactly that, and this class supplies it without reimplementing curve maths — each segment converts its local four-point window into an equivalent cubic Bezier and hands it to BezierUtils. The second half of the class solves the other problem nobody expects: the curve parameter t does not advance at a constant rate per unit of world distance, so a follower driven by t speeds up and slows down for no visible reason. BuildLengthTable plus DistanceToT converts distance into t, which is what makes constant-speed movement and evenly spaced props along a path possible.","workedExample":{"setup":"Three collinear points 5 units apart — the configuration pinned down by the self-test, plus a hand-derivation of why the length table exists at all.","inputs":[{"name":"points","value":"[(0,0,0), (5,0,0), (10,0,0)]"},{"name":"tension","value":"0 (default)"},{"name":"samplesPerSegment","value":"20 (default)"}],"steps":["BuildLengthTable produces (3 - 1) segments * 20 + 1 = 41 samples. The self-test asserts TotalLength(table) is within 0.05 of 10 — the true straight-line length — and that no entry ever decreases.","Evaluate(points, 0.5) resolves to segment 1 with localT 0, which returns that segment's Bezier b0 = points[1] exactly: (5, 0, 0). The self-test's third check confirms the same pass-through property on a curved four-point path at tension 0.3, within 0.01 of every control point.","Now the non-obvious part. Evaluate(points, 0.25) resolves to segment 0 with localT 0.5. That segment's window duplicates the first point, giving p0 = p1 = (0,0,0), p2 = (5,0,0), p3 = (10,0,0).","ToBezier at tension 0: tangentAtP1 = (p2 - p0)/2 = (2.5,0,0), tangentAtP2 = (p3 - p1)/2 = (5,0,0). So b0 = (0,0,0), b1 = (0.8333,0,0), b2 = (3.3333,0,0), b3 = (5,0,0).","BezierUtils.Cubic at localT 0.5 weights those four by 0.125 / 0.375 / 0.375 / 0.125: 0 + 0.375*0.8333 + 0.375*3.3333 + 0.125*5 = 0.3125 + 1.25 + 0.625 = 2.1875.","So t = 0.25 sits at x = 2.1875 — only 21.875% of the way along a perfectly straight 10-unit path.","DistanceToT(table, distance) is the fix: the self-test checks the two exact endpoints (distance 0 maps to t 0, distance == TotalLength maps to t 1) and that t never moves backward as distance is walked forward in 20 even steps."],"result":"Evaluate gives you the SHAPE; BuildLengthTable and DistanceToT give you the PACING. Uniform steps in t are not uniform in distance even on a dead-straight path, so anything that must move at constant speed or space objects evenly has to go through the table.","verifiedBy":"selftest:Math/Spline/Editor/SplineUtilsSelfTest.cs"},"useCases":["Camera rails and cutscene dollies authored by clicking waypoints in the scene view.","Patrol and racing lines where the AI must actually pass through the designer's marked points.","Placing props evenly along a path — fence posts, streetlights, track sections — using DistanceToT to space them by world distance rather than by t.","A follower that moves at a fixed metres-per-second along a curved path, advancing a distance value and converting it each frame.","Generating a smooth polyline for a LineRenderer or a procedural mesh from a handful of control points."],"usage":{"code":"// build once, when points or tension change\nfloat[] table = SplineUtils.BuildLengthTable(points, tension, samplesPerSegment: 20);\nfloat total = SplineUtils.TotalLength(table);\n\n// per frame: advance by distance, not by t\ntravelled = Mathf.Repeat(travelled + speed * Time.deltaTime, total);\nfloat t = SplineUtils.DistanceToT(table, travelled);\ntransform.position = SplineUtils.Evaluate(points, t, tension);\ntransform.rotation = Quaternion.LookRotation(SplineUtils.EvaluateTangent(points, t, tension));","codeNote":"The table is the only cacheable thing here; every other call is stateless and re-walks the point list."},"apiNotes":{"Evaluate(IReadOnlyList<Vector3>, float, float)":"Position at t in [0,1] across the WHOLE path (0 = first point, 1 = last). t is clamped internally. Returns Vector3.zero for an empty list.","EvaluateTangent(IReadOnlyList<Vector3>, float, float)":"Normalized direction of travel at t, via BezierUtils.CubicTangent. Returns Vector3.forward when there are fewer than 2 points.","BuildLengthTable(IReadOnlyList<Vector3>, float, int)":"Samples the path at uniform t and accumulates chord distances. table[0] is 0, table[last] is the total length. Allocates a float[] of segmentCount * samplesPerSegment + 1 — build it once and reuse it.","TotalLength(float[])":"Just the last entry of the table. Indexes directly, so an empty array throws IndexOutOfRange.","DistanceToT(float[], float)":"Binary-searches the table for the bracketing samples, then linearly interpolates between them. Clamps distance into [0, total]. This is the constant-speed conversion."},"gotchas":[{"title":"The length table must match the points and tension it was built from","body":"DistanceToT takes only the table — nothing ties it back to the point list, and nothing validates the pairing. Change a control point or the tension without rebuilding and you get plausible-looking but wrong t values, with no error. Rebuild whenever points, tension, or (for local-space points) the transform changes."},{"title":"Building the table is not free — do not do it per frame","body":"BuildLengthTable performs segmentCount * samplesPerSegment full Evaluate calls and allocates a float[]. At 10 points and the default 20 samples that is 180 curve evaluations and a 181-float allocation every time. Cache it; Evaluate and DistanceToT themselves are cheap enough for per-frame use."},{"title":"Degenerate inputs fail silently","body":"An empty point list makes Evaluate return Vector3.zero, and fewer than two points makes EvaluateTangent return Vector3.forward. A mis-wired path therefore manifests as 'everything is sitting at the origin facing +Z' rather than as an exception. Check points.Count yourself before trusting the output."},{"title":"tension is clamped to [0,1] and only loosens the curve","body":"ToBezier applies Mathf.Clamp01, so values outside the range do nothing extra. tension 0 is standard uniform Catmull-Rom, which overshoots outside the hull at sharp corners; tension 1 collapses each segment to a straight line between its two points (still passing through all of them). There is no setting tighter than Catmull-Rom — negative tension is clamped away."},{"title":"Endpoints are clamped, and there is no closed-loop mode","body":"The missing neighbour at each end is faked by duplicating the endpoint, so the curve leaves the first point aimed straight at the second and arrives at the last the same way. Repeating the first point at the end of the list does NOT give a smooth loop — it gives a corner there."},{"title":"Length is a chord approximation and under-reads on tight curves","body":"The table sums straight-line distances between samples, which always cuts the corner. On gentle curves the error is negligible; on tight ones raise samplesPerSegment (at linear cost in build time and memory) until the measured length stops changing."}],"seeAlso":["BezierUtils","SplinePath","SplineFollower","PathBuilder","MathUtils"],"src_uses":["BezierUtils","PathBuilder","SplinePath"],"src_usedBy":["SplinePath"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"geometry-utils2d","name":"GeometryUtils2D","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Geometry2D/GeometryUtils2D.cs","src_lines":[7,53],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"2D geometry queries that don't need Physics2D colliders — cheap hit-tests/path validation for gameplay logic driven by plain Vector2 data (e.g. a hand-authored polygon, not a PolygonCollider2D).","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"ClosestPointOnSegment(Vector2, Vector2, Vector2)","sig":"public static Vector2 ClosestPointOnSegment(Vector2 point, Vector2 a, Vector2 b)","kind":"method","name":"ClosestPointOnSegment","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"SegmentsIntersect(Vector2, Vector2, Vector2, Vector2, out Vector2)","sig":"public static bool SegmentsIntersect(Vector2 a1, Vector2 a2, Vector2 b1, Vector2 b2, out Vector2 intersection)","kind":"method","name":"SegmentsIntersect","static":true,"comment":"Returns true and the intersection point if segments a1-a2 and b1-b2 cross."},{"id":"PointInPolygon(Vector2, Vector2[])","sig":"public static bool PointInPolygon(Vector2 point, Vector2[] polygon)","kind":"method","name":"PointInPolygon","static":true,"comment":"Ray-casting (even-odd rule) point-in-polygon test — works for any simple (non-self-intersecting) polygon, convex or concave."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":[],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Closest-point-on-segment, segment intersection and point-in-polygon tests on raw Vector2 data, without Physics2D.","problem":"Plenty of gameplay questions are geometric but have nothing to do with the physics world: is this waypoint inside the hand-authored patrol region stored in a ScriptableObject, does the path the player just drew cross itself, how far is the unit from that wall line. Answering those with Physics2D means creating GameObjects and colliders purely so you can call OverlapPoint, paying a physics sync, and binding the answer to layer and Rigidbody2D setup. These three functions answer the same questions directly on Vector2 values — no scene objects, no allocations, no frame-timing dependency, and usable from editor tooling and unit tests where there is no physics world at all.","workedExample":{"setup":"An L-shaped room and a point sitting in the notch — the case a naive convex test gets wrong.","inputs":[{"name":"polygon","value":"[(0,0), (4,0), (4,4), (2,4), (2,2), (0,2)]"},{"name":"point","value":"(1, 3)"}],"steps":["The test walks each edge (pj -> pi) and counts how many cross the horizontal ray shooting in +x from the point. An edge counts when the point's y sits between the edge's endpoints AND the edge's x at that y is to the RIGHT of the point.","Edge (0,2)->(0,0): both endpoint y values are below 3, so no crossing.","Edge (0,0)->(4,0): both below 3, no crossing.","Edge (4,0)->(4,4): 0 and 4 straddle y = 3. x at that height is 4, and 1 < 4, so this counts. inside = true.","Edge (4,4)->(2,4): both y values are 4, above 3 — no straddle, no crossing.","Edge (2,4)->(2,2): 4 and 2 straddle y = 3. x at that height is 2, and 1 < 2, so this counts too. inside = false.","Edge (2,2)->(0,2): both at y = 2, below 3 — no crossing.","Two crossings — an even number — so the point is outside."],"result":"PointInPolygon returns false. The ray leaves the notch, enters the L's upright and exits its right wall: two crossings, therefore outside. A convex hull test on the same polygon would have wrongly returned true.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Validating that a placement click landed inside a hand-drawn buildable zone before spending resources.","Checking that a newly drawn path segment does not cross any segment already drawn (drawing puzzles, rope/wire routing, no-crossing rules).","Distance-to-wall queries for steering or nudging, using ClosestPointOnSegment against a list of wall lines.","Editor tooling that must answer geometric questions on serialized Vector2[] data with no scene loaded.","Minimap and map-region hit testing where the region is a designer-authored polygon, not a collider."],"usage":{"code":"Vector2 nearest = GeometryUtils2D.ClosestPointOnSegment(playerPos, wallStart, wallEnd);\nfloat gap = Vector2.Distance(playerPos, nearest);\n\nif (GeometryUtils2D.SegmentsIntersect(newA, newB, oldA, oldB, out Vector2 hit))\n    Debug.Log($\"paths cross at {hit}\");\n\nbool allowed = GeometryUtils2D.PointInPolygon(clickPos, buildZone);","codeNote":"All three are static and allocation-free; the polygon is a plain Vector2[] you can serialize on a MonoBehaviour or ScriptableObject."},"apiNotes":{"ClosestPointOnSegment(Vector2, Vector2, Vector2)":"Projects the point onto the infinite line through a and b, then clamps the parameter to [0,1] so the answer is always ON the segment, never past an endpoint. Returns a when a and b are within ~0.0003 units of each other.","SegmentsIntersect(Vector2, Vector2, Vector2, Vector2, out Vector2)":"Cross-product parametric solve. Returns false for parallel or collinear pairs (the determinant test), and true with the crossing point only when both parameters land in [0,1].","PointInPolygon(Vector2, Vector2[])":"Even-odd ray casting. Works for concave polygons and ignores winding direction, so you do not have to author vertices clockwise."},"gotchas":[{"title":"Collinear overlapping segments report NO intersection","body":"SegmentsIntersect bails out when the determinant is near zero, which covers both genuinely parallel segments AND segments lying exactly on top of each other. If you are using it to validate \"this new path segment must not cross an existing one\", a segment that doubles straight back along an existing one passes the check."},{"title":"Shared endpoints count as an intersection","body":"The parameter test is inclusive (t and u may be exactly 0 or 1), so two segments that merely touch at a corner report true. Building a closed polyline, every consecutive pair of edges will 'intersect'. Skip adjacent pairs explicitly when validating a chain."},{"title":"PointInPolygon assumes a simple polygon and closes it for you","body":"The loop wraps from the last vertex back to the first, so do NOT repeat the opening vertex at the end of the array. Self-intersecting (figure-of-eight) polygons are outside the contract — the even-odd rule will carve holes out of the overlap region rather than erroring."},{"title":"Vector3 to Vector2 keeps X and Y, not X and Z","body":"Unity's implicit conversion drops Z. In a top-down game whose ground plane is XZ, passing a world position straight in silently collapses everything onto the vertical axis. Convert explicitly with new Vector2(v.x, v.z) at the call site."},{"title":"PointInPolygon is O(n) per query, with no spatial acceleration","body":"Fine for a few dozen vertices tested a few times a frame. Testing hundreds of agents against a hundred-vertex polygon every frame is not what this is for — do a bounding-box rejection first, or use real colliders."}],"seeAlso":["RaycastUtils","MathUtils","PathBuilder","GridPlacer"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":[],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"math-utils","name":"MathUtils","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/MathUtils/MathUtils.cs","src_lines":[5,61],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Mod(int, int)","sig":"public static int Mod(int a, int b)","kind":"method","name":"Mod","static":true,"comment":"Proper mathematical modulo, unlike C#'s % operator, which returns negative results for negative input. Mod(-1, 5) => 4, not -1. Useful for wrapping array indices, angles, and grid coordinates."},{"id":"Mod(float, float)","sig":"public static float Mod(float a, float b)","kind":"method","name":"Mod","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"Remap(float, float, float, float, float)","sig":"public static float Remap(float value, float inMin, float inMax, float outMin, float outMax)","kind":"method","name":"Remap","static":true,"comment":"Remaps a value from one range to another, e.g. Remap(healthPercent, 0, 1, minPitch, maxPitch)."},{"id":"RemapClamped(float, float, float, float, float)","sig":"public static float RemapClamped(float value, float inMin, float inMax, float outMin, float outMax)","kind":"method","name":"RemapClamped","static":true,"comment":"Same as Remap but clamps the input first, avoids overshoot when value is slightly out of range."},{"id":"DeltaAngle(float, float)","sig":"public static float DeltaAngle(float fromDegrees, float toDegrees)","kind":"method","name":"DeltaAngle","static":true,"comment":"Shortest signed difference between two angles in degrees, always in [-180, 180]. Use this instead of subtracting angles directly to avoid the 359-to-1-degree wraparound bug."},{"id":"Damp(float, float, float, float)","sig":"public static float Damp(float current, float target, float sharpness, float deltaTime)","kind":"method","name":"Damp","static":true,"comment":"Frame-rate independent smoothing, exponential decay toward target. Higher `sharpness` = snappier response."},{"id":"Damp(Vector3, Vector3, float, float)","sig":"public static Vector3 Damp(Vector3 current, Vector3 target, float sharpness, float deltaTime)","kind":"method","name":"Damp","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"InRange(float, float, float)","sig":"public static bool InRange(float value, float min, float max)","kind":"method","name":"InRange","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"DirectionTo(Vector3, Vector3)","sig":"public static Vector3 DirectionTo(Vector3 from, Vector3 to)","kind":"method","name":"DirectionTo","static":true,"comment":"Direction and distance packed together, saves a redundant (b - a).normalized elsewhere."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathAndUI_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Scalar helpers Unity omits or gets surprising: true modulo, range remapping, angle deltas, frame-rate-independent damping.","problem":"A short list of one-liners that are individually trivial and collectively responsible for a lot of bugs. C#'s % returns negative results for negative operands, so wrapping an index backwards off the start of an array breaks. Subtracting two angles blows up across the 359-to-1 degree seam. And the near-universal Lerp(current, target, 0.1f) smoothing idiom silently changes speed with frame rate, so the game feels different at 30 and 144 fps. Each function here is the correct version of something people write wrong from memory.","workedExample":{"setup":"Proving Damp is genuinely frame-rate independent: damp a value from 0 toward 10 with sharpness 5, once at 30 fps and twice at 60 fps. Both must land on the same number.","inputs":[{"name":"current","value":"0"},{"name":"target","value":"10"},{"name":"sharpness","value":"5"}],"steps":["The formula is Mathf.Lerp(current, target, 1 - Mathf.Exp(-sharpness * deltaTime)).","One frame at deltaTime = 1/30: 1 - exp(-5/30) = 1 - exp(-0.1666667) = 1 - 0.8464817 = 0.1535183. Lerp(0, 10, 0.1535183) = 1.535183.","One frame at deltaTime = 1/60: 1 - exp(-5/60) = 1 - exp(-0.0833333) = 1 - 0.9200444 = 0.0799556.","60 fps step 1: Lerp(0, 10, 0.0799556) = 0.799556.","60 fps step 2: 0.799556 + (10 - 0.799556) * 0.0799556 = 0.799556 + 0.735627 = 1.535183.","They match because the remaining gap multiplies by exp(-sharpness*dt) each frame, and exp(-5/60) squared is exactly exp(-5/30). The naive Lerp(current, target, 0.1f) has no such property: two steps leave 0.81 of the gap, one step leaves 0.9."],"result":"1.535183 either way. Whatever the frame rate, the same wall-clock time produces the same amount of smoothing — which is the entire reason to use Damp instead of a raw Lerp factor.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Wrapping a selection index in a menu or inventory: Mod(index - 1, count) hits the last item instead of -1.","Turning a character toward a heading without the 359-to-1 wraparound spin: DeltaAngle gives the shortest signed turn.","Smoothing a camera, a health bar or an aim reticle toward a target with Damp, so it behaves identically on every machine.","Driving audio or VFX parameters from gameplay values: Remap(healthPercent, 0, 1, minPitch, maxPitch).","Any place a normalized 0-1 value needs to become a designer-facing range and back again."],"usage":{"code":"transform.position = MathUtils.Damp(transform.position, target.position, 8f, Time.deltaTime);\nfloat turn = MathUtils.DeltaAngle(transform.eulerAngles.y, desiredYaw);\nint previous = MathUtils.Mod(index - 1, items.Length);\nfloat pitch = MathUtils.RemapClamped(healthPercent, 0f, 1f, 0.8f, 1.2f);","codeNote":"Damp has both a float and a Vector3 overload, so the same call reads the same whether you are smoothing a number or a position."},"apiNotes":{"Mod(int, int)":"(a % b + b) % b — the mathematical modulo. Mod(-1, 5) is 4, where C#'s -1 % 5 is -1. The go-to for wrapping indices, grid coordinates and angles.","Mod(float, float)":"Same identity for floats. Used internally by DeltaAngle.","Remap(float, float, float, float, float)":"Rescales value from [inMin, inMax] onto [outMin, outMax]. Does NOT clamp: out-of-range input extrapolates past the output bounds.","RemapClamped(float, float, float, float, float)":"Same, but via Mathf.InverseLerp, which clamps the normalized parameter to [0,1] first. The safer default.","DeltaAngle(float, float)":"Shortest signed turn in degrees, always in [-180, 180]. DeltaAngle(350, 10) = Mod(10 - 350 + 180, 360) - 180 = 200 - 180 = +20, not -340.","Damp(float, float, float, float)":"Exponential decay toward target, framerate independent. sharpness is a rate, not a duration — see the worked example and the gotcha below.","Damp(Vector3, Vector3, float, float)":"Same curve applied component-wise via Vector3.Lerp. Use it for positions; the whole vector converges at one rate.","InRange(float, float, float)":"Inclusive at both ends: value >= min && value <= max. Nothing stops you passing min > max, which just always returns false.","DirectionTo(Vector3, Vector3)":"(to - from).normalized. Purely a readability helper — it does not also hand back the distance, despite what the source comment about 'direction and distance packed together' implies."},"gotchas":[{"title":"sharpness is a rate, not a time","body":"Damp closes 1 - exp(-sharpness) of the remaining gap per second: sharpness 5 is about 99.3% closed after one second, sharpness 1 about 63%. Higher is snappier. There is no 'reaches the target in N seconds' setting — exponential decay never actually arrives, it just gets arbitrarily close, so add an explicit snap if you need exact equality."},{"title":"Remap divides by (inMax - inMin) with no guard","body":"Equal input bounds give a division by zero, which for floats silently yields Infinity or NaN and propagates through everything downstream. RemapClamped routes through Mathf.InverseLerp, which returns 0 for a degenerate range instead. Prefer RemapClamped unless you specifically want extrapolation outside the input range."},{"title":"Mod by zero behaves differently for int and float","body":"The int overload throws DivideByZeroException (C# integer semantics); the float overload silently returns NaN. Both mirror the underlying % operator — neither is validated here."},{"title":"DirectionTo returns zero, not NaN, when from == to","body":"Unity's .normalized returns Vector3.zero for a zero-length vector rather than producing NaN. That is safer, but Quaternion.LookRotation still rejects a zero forward vector with an error. Check sqrMagnitude before using the result to build a rotation."},{"title":"DeltaAngle duplicates UnityEngine.Mathf.DeltaAngle","body":"Not a trap, just worth knowing: this is functionally the same as Unity's built-in. It exists so the toolkit's angle handling is visible and self-contained on top of its own Mod. Either is fine; do not assume this one does something extra."}],"seeAlso":["EasingFunctions","GeometryUtils2D","NoiseUtils","SmoothFollowCamera","StatCurves"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":["CameraFollowMode","PlayerCameraMode","PlayerCameraRig","SmoothFollowCamera","SplineFollowMode","SplineFollower"],"src_extensionPoints":[]},{"id":"noise-utils","name":"NoiseUtils","cat":"Math","src_typeKind":"class","src_ns":"Toolkit.MathUtilities","src_file":"Math/Noise/NoiseUtils.cs","src_lines":[9,39],"src_base":null,"src_implements":[],"src_static":true,"src_internal":false,"src_runtime":true,"src_attributes":{},"src_typeComment":"Thin convenience layer over Unity's built-in Mathf.PerlinNoise — a single octave of Perlin noise looks fairly uniform/smooth; layering several octaves at increasing frequency and decreasing amplitude (aka Fractal Brownian Motion) gives the more natural, detailed look most \"noise-driven\" effects actually want (camera shake, wind sway, terrain-ish variation).","src_serialized":[],"src_members":[{"id":"Fbm(float, float, int, float, float)","sig":"public static float Fbm(float x, float y, int octaves = 4, float lacunarity = 2f, float gain = 0.5f)","kind":"method","name":"Fbm","static":true,"comment":null},{"id":"Fbm1D(float, int, float, float)","sig":"public static float Fbm1D(float x, int octaves = 4, float lacunarity = 2f, float gain = 0.5f)","kind":"method","name":"Fbm1D","static":true,"comment":"1D convenience overload (e.g. Fbm1D(time) for a wandering value) — samples 2D Perlin along a fixed second axis, since Unity has no native 1D Perlin function."}],"src_enumValues":[],"src_selfTest":null,"src_demoScene":["MathFormulas_Demo","ProceduralGeneration_Demo"],"entryKind":"tool","status":"published","summary":"Fractal Brownian Motion over Mathf.PerlinNoise — stacked octaves, normalized back to 0-1 whatever the octave count.","problem":"A single octave of Perlin noise is smooth to the point of being bland: rolling hills with no detail, a camera shake that drifts rather than rattles. The standard fix is Fractal Brownian Motion — sum several octaves, each at double the frequency and half the amplitude of the last, so you get large-scale shape plus fine-scale detail. The arithmetic is short but easy to get subtly wrong, and the part people usually skip is the normalization: without dividing by the total amplitude, changing the octave count changes the output's range and average, so every consumer downstream has to be rebalanced. This does that division for you.","workedExample":{"setup":"Tracing the amplitude bookkeeping at the default settings, and why the final division is the feature.","inputs":[{"name":"octaves","value":"4"},{"name":"lacunarity","value":"2"},{"name":"gain","value":"0.5"}],"steps":["Octave 0: amplitude 1, frequency 1. maxPossible = 1.","Octave 1: amplitude 0.5, frequency 2. maxPossible = 1.5.","Octave 2: amplitude 0.25, frequency 4. maxPossible = 1.75.","Octave 3: amplitude 0.125, frequency 8. maxPossible = 1.875.","Mathf.PerlinNoise returns roughly [0,1], so the raw total lives in [0, 1.875]. The final `return total / maxPossible` maps that back onto [0,1].","Concretely: if every octave happened to return 0.5, total = 0.5 * 1.875 = 0.9375, and 0.9375 / 1.875 = 0.5 exactly.","With octaves = 8 the amplitudes run 1, 0.5, ... 0.0078125 and maxPossible = 1.9921875 — a different divisor, but the same mid-value comes back out."],"result":"Octave count changes the DETAIL of the field but not its range or its centre. You can raise octaves from 4 to 8 to make terrain rougher without retuning the height multiplier or the shake amplitude that consumes the value.","verifiedBy":"derivation"},"useCases":["Camera or weapon shake that reads as handheld rather than as a sine wave, by sampling Fbm1D against Time.time.","Wind sway on foliage, banners and hanging signs, with a per-object offset so nothing moves in lockstep.","Heightmap and terrain-ish variation: Fbm(x * scale, z * scale) as a 0-1 height factor.","Flicker for torches and damaged lights — smoother and less repetitive than Random.value per frame.","Scattering density masks for grass, rocks or spawn points, thresholding the noise value."],"usage":{"code":"// wandering value in -0.5 .. 0.5, per-object offset keeps things out of sync\nfloat sway = NoiseUtils.Fbm1D(Time.time * 0.5f + phaseOffset) - 0.5f;\ntransform.localRotation = Quaternion.Euler(0f, 0f, sway * 10f);\n\nfloat height = NoiseUtils.Fbm(worldX * 0.05f, worldZ * 0.05f, octaves: 5);","codeNote":"Scale the input coordinates — the frequency of the first octave is 1, so unscaled world coordinates change far too fast."},"apiNotes":{"Fbm(float, float, int, float, float)":"The real implementation. lacunarity multiplies the frequency each octave (2 = each layer twice as fine), gain multiplies the amplitude (0.5 = each layer half as loud). octaves is clamped to at least 1 on entry, so a zero or negative value degrades to a single octave rather than returning NaN. Result is normalized to 0-1.","Fbm1D(float, int, float, float)":"Calls Fbm(x, 0.5f, ...) — a fixed slice through the 2D field, because Unity has no 1D Perlin. Use it for anything that varies over time alone."},"gotchas":[{"title":"octaves is clamped to 1, so a zero never reaches the divide","body":"With octaves <= 0 the layering loop never runs, maxPossible stays 0f, and the normalizing divide at the bottom would be 0f / 0f — a NaN that then silently poisons whatever height, shake offset or colour consumed it. Fbm now clamps octaves to a minimum of 1 before the loop. A serialized field left at 0 therefore produces plain single-octave Perlin, which looks wrong but is debuggable, instead of a NaN that propagates."},{"title":"Sample on whole-number coordinates and you get a flat field","body":"Perlin is gradient noise defined on an integer lattice, so whole-number inputs land exactly on lattice points and the first octave degenerates to a constant. Iterating a grid as Fbm(x, y) with integer x and y is the classic mistake — always scale: Fbm(x * 0.05f, y * 0.05f)."},{"title":"Fbm1D's fixed second axis lands on the lattice for octaves 2 and up","body":"It passes y = 0.5, and y is multiplied by frequency each octave, giving 0.5, 1, 2, 4 at the defaults — so every octave after the first samples exactly along an integer row. The output still varies along x, but it is a slightly less lively slice than an arbitrary y would give. If a wander looks repetitive, call Fbm(x, someNonIntegerOffset) directly instead."},{"title":"Perlin is not random and it is not seedable","body":"Mathf.PerlinNoise is a deterministic function of its coordinates with no seed parameter, and it is symmetric about the origin. The only way to get a different-looking field is to offset your input coordinates — keep a per-instance or per-level offset and add it to x and y. Repeated calls with the same arguments always return the same value."},{"title":"Output is centred near 0.5, not 0","body":"For a bidirectional effect (shake, sway) subtract 0.5 and scale, as the usage snippet does. Feeding the raw value into a position offset biases everything in one direction."},{"title":"Cost scales linearly with octaves","body":"Each octave is a full Mathf.PerlinNoise call. Four octaves is four times the cost of one. Sampling this per-vertex or per-pixel on the CPU adds up fast — for large fields, bake to a texture or move the maths to a shader."}],"seeAlso":["CameraShake","TerrainHeightmapGenerator","SeededRandom","GrassBendEmitter","MathUtils"],"src_uses":[],"src_usedBy":["TerrainHeightmapGenerator"],"src_extensionPoints":[]}],"groups":[{"title":"Randomness & Probability","ids":["probability-utils","seeded-random","random-bag","pity-timer"]},{"title":"Curves & Easing","ids":["easing-functions","ease-type","stat-curves"]},{"title":"Splines & Geometry","ids":["bezier-utils","spline-utils","geometry-utils2d"]},{"title":"Scalars & Noise","ids":["math-utils","noise-utils"]}]};
